(>> Solution to Question 10:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze article usage before mass nouns that take post-modifying 'of'-phrases in scientific descriptions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Generic vs. Specific Mass Noun Contrast:) Tj
T*
(   An uncountable mass noun standing alone in a generic statement takes ZERO article. However, as) Tj
T*
(   soon as that mass noun is restricted, qualified, or bounded by an 'of'-prepositional phrase, it) Tj
T*
(   ceases to be generic and MUST take the definite article 'THE':) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Side-by-Side Diagnostic Case Studies:) Tj
T*
(   - Case 1 \(Protein\):) Tj
T*
(   - Generic \(Zero Article\): "Protein folding is governed by thermodynamic stability.") Tj
T*
(   - Restricted \(Definite Article\): "The protein of the outer membrane was characterized.") Tj
T*
(   - Case 2 \(DNA\):) Tj
T*
(   - Generic \(Zero Article\): "DNA is transcribed into messenger RNA.") Tj
T*
(   - Restricted \(Definite Article\): "The DNA of the pathogenic isolate was sequenced.") Tj
T*
(   - Case 3 \(Purity\):) Tj
T*
(   - Generic \(Zero Article\): "Purity is essential in clinical injectable formulations.") Tj
T*
(   - Restricted \(Definite Article\): "The purity of the synthesized oligonucleotide exceeded 99%.") Tj
T*
(   - Case 4 \(Kinetics\):) Tj
T*
(   - Generic \(Zero Article\): "Enzyme kinetics can be modeled via hyperbolic curves.") Tj
T*
(   - Restricted \(Definite Article\): "The kinetics of the mutant polymerase were evaluated at) Tj
T*
(   varying temperatures.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. The Universal Heuristic:) Tj
T*
(   Whenever an abstract or mass noun is followed by "of + [specific entity]", insert the definite) Tj
T*
(   article: "The [Noun] of [Entity]".) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "The efficiency of transfection", "The viscosity of the broth", "The stability of) Tj
T*
(   the vector", "The yield of recombinant biomass".) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 11:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine determiner usage with 'Much', 'Many', 'A lot of', and 'Plenty of' across formal and informal scientific registers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Register Stratification of Quantifiers:) Tj
T*
(   In academic and scientific prose, quantifiers carry distinct stylistic registers that impact) Tj
T*
(   peer-review credibility:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Formal Scientific Quantifiers:) Tj
T*
(   - Much: Modifies uncountable mass nouns in negative clauses and questions; in affirmative) Tj
T*
(   clauses, prefer 'a substantial amount of', 'significant', or 'considerable':) Tj
T*
(   - Informal: "The process took much energy.") Tj
T*
(   - Formal Academic: "The process required a substantial amount of energy.") Tj
T*
(   - Formal Negative: "We did not observe much variation across technical replicates.") Tj
T*
(   - Many: Modifies countable plural nouns across affirmative, negative, and interrogative) Tj
T*
(   clauses:) Tj
T*
(   - "Many mutant strains exhibited altered growth kinetics.") Tj
T*
(   - Academic Alternative: "Numerous mutant strains exhibited altered growth kinetics.") Tj
T*
(   - Academic Alternative: "A substantial number of candidates were screened.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. Informal / Colloquial Quantifiers to Avoid:) Tj
T*
ET

endstream
