/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the syntax and semantics of 'All', 'Whole', and 'Entire' in biological descriptions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Structural Differences in Determiner Positioning:) Tj
T*
(   - 'All' is a predeterminer. It precedes definite articles, possessives, and demonstratives:) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: All + [the / my / these] + [Plural Noun / Mass Noun].) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "All the cells were lysed.") Tj
T*
(   - Example: "All the DNA was extracted.") Tj
T*
(   - 'Whole' and 'Entire' are central adjectives. They MUST follow articles and possessive) Tj
T*
(   determiners:) Tj
T*
(   - Formula: [The / A / My] + [whole / entire] + [Singular Countable Noun].) Tj
T*
(   - Example: "The whole organism was examined.") Tj
T*
(   - Example: "The entire genome was assembled de novo.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Common Ungrammatical Flaws:) Tj
T*
(   - Ungrammatical: "*Whole the genome was sequenced.") Tj
T*
(   - Ungrammatical: "*All genome was sequenced.") Tj
T*
(   - Grammatical: "The entire genome was sequenced" OR "All of the genome was sequenced.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 13:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine article usage before acronyms, abbreviations, and symbols \(e.g., pH, UV, DNA, mRNA\) in biotechnology.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Acronym vs. Initialism Linguistic Distinction:) Tj
T*
(   - Initialisms \(Spelled out letter by letter\): Pronunciation of the first letter governs article) Tj
T*
(   selection:) Tj
T*
(   - UV \(You-Vee -> Consonant glide /j/\): Takes 'A' \("a UV spectrophotometer"\).) Tj
T*
(   - MRI \(Em-Ar-Eye -> Vowel sound /e/\): Takes 'An' \("an MRI scan"\).) Tj
T*
(   - NGO \(En-Gee-Oh -> Vowel sound /e/\): Takes 'An' \("an NGO grant"\).) Tj
T*
(   - Acronyms \(Pronounced as an ordinary word\):) Tj
T*
(   - CRISPR \(Pronounced 'kris-per' -> Consonant /k/\): Takes 'A' \("a CRISPR gene-editing vector"\).) Tj
T*
(   - ELISA \(Pronounced 'ee-ly-zuh' -> Vowel /i:/\): Takes 'An' \("an ELISA plate"\).) Tj
T*
(   - AIDS \(Pronounced 'aydz' -> Vowel /ei/\): Takes 'An' \("an AIDS vaccine"\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Symbols and Zero Article:) Tj
T*
(   - The chemical symbol 'pH' is treated as a continuous physical parameter and takes zero article) Tj
T*
(   or definite article depending on specificity:) Tj
T*
(   - "Maintain pH at 7.0." \(Zero article\).) Tj
T*
(   - "The pH of the growth medium fluctuated." \(Definite article\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 14:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the omission of articles in paired idioms, parallel structures, and tabular lab protocols.) Tj
T*
