(   [Problem Statement]: Examine article usage before names of diseases, viruses, bacteria, and clinical medical conditions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Infectious and Chronic Medical Pathologies:) Tj
T*
(   Standard contemporary medical and biological English utilizes ZERO article before names of) Tj
T*
(   diseases:) Tj
T*
(   - "The patient contracted tuberculosis." \(Never *the tuberculosis\).) Tj
T*
(   - "CRISPR therapies are being developed to treat sickle cell anemia.") Tj
T*
(   - "Diabetes mellitus affects glucose homeostasis.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Viral and Bacterial Taxa vs. Clinical Syndromes:) Tj
T*
(   - Taxonomic Organism \(Capitalized binomial or abbreviated taxon\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Escherichia coli", "Mycobacterium tuberculosis", "SARS-CoV-2". \(Zero article\).) Tj
T*
(   - The Virus / The Bacterium \(Noun adjunct modifying common noun\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The SARS-CoV-2 virus", "The E. coli bacterium", "The influenza virus". \(Takes 'The' because) Tj
T*
(   'virus/bacterium' is the common head noun\).) Tj
T*
(   - Clinical Syndromes:) Tj
T*
(   - "COVID-19" \(Zero article\) vs. "The COVID-19 pandemic" \(Takes 'the' modifying 'pandemic'\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
