/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 18:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the grammatical rules of 'Some' versus 'Any' in scientific hypotheses, negative findings, and controls.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Affirmative Declaratives \('Some'\):) Tj
T*
(   - Used in positive statements to denote an unspecified, positive quantity:) Tj
T*
(   - "We detected some degradation in lane 4.") Tj
T*
(   - "Some mutant colonies exhibited ampicillin resistance.") Tj
T*
(   - Used in polite offers or affirmative inquiries expecting assent:) Tj
T*
(   - "Would you like some additional lysis buffer?") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Non-Affirmative Contexts \('Any'\):) Tj
T*
(   - Used in negative sentences, conditional 'if'-clauses, and open questions:) Tj
T*
(   - Negative finding: "The negative control did not produce any fluorescent signal.") Tj
T*
(   - Conditional: "If you detect any precipitation, re-heat the mixture to 37 deg C.") Tj
T*
(   - Question: "Did the spectrophotometer register any absorbance at 260 nm?") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. 'Any' in Affirmative Clauses \(Meaning 'No Matter Which'\):) Tj
T*
(   - When 'any' is stressed in an affirmative statement, it signifies unrestricted free choice:) Tj
T*
(   - "Any calibrated micropipette can dispense this volume." \(It does not matter which one you) Tj
T*
(   select\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
