(>> Solution to Question 28:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine the hazards of overusing 'the' \(article clutter\) in scientific methodology sections.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. The Phenomenon of Article Clutter:) Tj
T*
(   Novice writers frequently over-insert 'the' before every noun, creating clunky, pedantic, and) Tj
T*
(   exhausting prose:) Tj
T*
(   - Cluttered: "The centrifugation of the cells at the speed of 10,000 rpm resulted in the) Tj
T*
(   sedimentation of the pellet at the bottom of the tube.") Tj
T*
(   - Diagnosis: Seven instances of 'the' smother the procedural momentum.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Streamlined Academic Editing:) Tj
T*
(   - Eliminate unnecessary articles by converting prepositional nominalizations into direct active) Tj
T*
(   or passive verbal clauses:) Tj
T*
(   - Streamlined: "Centrifuging cells at 10,000 rpm pelleted the debris at the tube bottom.") Tj
T*
(   - Concision: Reduces word count by 40%, enhances clarity, and elevates professional impact.) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 29:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the syntactic distribution of 'Enough' as a determiner, adjective, and adverb in bioengineering.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Syntactic Mobility of 'Enough':) Tj
T*
(   The word 'enough' exhibits unique positional rules depending on its grammatical part of speech:) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Rules of Placement:) Tj
T*
(   - As a Determiner \(Precedes Nouns\):) Tj
T*
(   - "We have enough substrate to conduct three technical replicates.") Tj
T*
(   - "Did the bioreactor accumulate enough dissolved oxygen?") Tj
T*
(   - Note: While archaic English permitted post-nominal placement \("substrate enough"\), modern) Tj
T*
(   scientific prose strictly places 'enough' before the noun.) Tj
T*
(   - As an Adverb \(Follows Adjectives, Adverbs, and Verbs\):) Tj
T*
(   - Must follow the modified adjective:) Tj
T*
(   - "The sensor is not sensitive enough to detect femtomolar concentrations." \(Never *enough) Tj
T*
(   sensitive\).) Tj
T*
(   - "The autoclave was not heated quickly enough.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 30:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine common translation transfer errors in article usage by non-native scientific researchers.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Linguistic Interference from Article-Free Languages:) Tj
T*
(   Researchers whose native tongues lack articles \(e.g., Hindi, Russian, Chinese, Japanese,) Tj
T*
(   Korean\) systematically face two major transfer errors in academic English:) Tj
T*
(   - Error 1: Article Under-Use \(Omission of mandatory articles before singular countable nouns\):) Tj
T*
(   - Flawed: "*Researcher prepared plasmid in laboratory.") Tj
T*
(   - Correction: "The researcher prepared the plasmid in the laboratory.") Tj
T*
(   - Rule: A singular countable noun in English CANNOT stand alone without a determiner \(a, an,) Tj
T*
(   the, this, my\).) Tj
T*
ET

